ENG-11, ENG-14  A stream opened on the default output or input device moves with it: headphones or a headset microphone plugged in take over.
Merged 2026-11-15 from 0001-winecoreaudio-follow-default-output.patch (ENG-11) and 0002-winecoreaudio-follow-default-input.patch (ENG-14); the tree they give is the same.

dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c (winecoreaudio.so). Lullaby, 2026-10-27 and 2026-10-28. LGPL-2.1-or-later like Wine.

== ENG-11 (output) ==
dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c (winecoreaudio.so). Lullaby, 2026-10-27. LGPL-2.1-or-later like Wine.

What: the driver binds a stream's audio unit to one device (kAudioOutputUnitProperty_CurrentDevice) when the stream is opened and
never looks again, so a game that has no choice of audio device keeps playing on the speakers after headphones are plugged in
(Windows moves such streams itself). Now a render stream whose device is the default output device when it is opened follows it:
one listener (kAudioHardwarePropertyDefaultOutputDevice) only counts the changes; the stream notices the count at its next call
from the game (get_current_padding, get_render_buffer) or in its timer thread, makes a new audio unit for the new default device the
way the first one was made, stops the old unit, puts the new one in its place and starts it. The buffers, position and volume are
not touched, so the sound goes on from where it was after a gap of a few milliseconds. A stream opened on another device than the
default, a capture stream and an exclusive choice of the game are left alone.
Why on the game's thread and not in the listener: in Resident Evil Requiem a question to CoreAudio from the listener's thread
(the device's buffer sizes, a unit's device) was never answered, so the move never finished and the stream stayed on the
unplugged device (silence). The same questions from the thread the stream was made on are answered.
Also: ca_render_cb no longer waits for the stream's lock (silence for that buffer if it is taken): get_latency holds the lock while
it asks CoreAudio about the device, and a callback waiting there can end in a circle with a unit being stopped.
Other bases: the driver is Wine's own; the same change applies to any Wine with winecoreaudio.drv (WineForge too, by hand).
Test: patches/engine/tests/wasapi-default-follow.c plays a quiet tone on the default device; with WINEDEBUG=+coreaudio the driver says
"output device A -> B" when the default output changes (plug headphones in and out, or switch the output in System Settings).
Verified on: Dormison r7, built in scratch/local-bridge/dormison-r7 (2026-10-27): Resident Evil Requiem, default output switched from the
speakers to the headphones and back, both moves done; heard by the owner on other games; Isaac, ETS 2, PEAK not rechecked after the last change.
Switch: LULLABY_AUDIO_FOLLOW=0 as a launch option turns it off for a run; on by default (a fix of a plain fault).


== ENG-14 (input) ==
dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c (winecoreaudio.so). Lullaby, 2026-10-28. LGPL-2.1-or-later like Wine.

What: ENG-11 for the other direction. The driver binds a capture stream's audio unit to one microphone when the stream is opened,
so a game with voice chat keeps listening to the built-in microphone after a headset is plugged in (Windows moves such streams
itself). Now a capture stream whose device is the default input device when it is opened follows it: a listener on
kAudioHardwarePropertyDefaultInputDevice counts the changes (one per direction, the render one is ENG-11's); the stream notices the
count at its next call from the game (get_capture_buffer, get_next_packet_size, get_current_padding) or in its timer thread, makes a
new audio unit and sample-rate converter for the new microphone the way the first ones were made, stops the old unit, puts the new
ones in their place and starts it. The ring of captured frames starts empty (its frames were the old device's, at its rate), the
frames already handed to the game and the volume are not touched. A stream on another device than the default and an exclusive
choice of the game are left alone.
Also: ca_capture_cb no longer waits for the stream's lock (that buffer of input is dropped if it is taken), for the same reason as
ca_render_cb in ENG-11.
Requires: nothing (the two parts share default_device(flow) and follow_check by direction).
Other bases: the driver is Wine's own; applies to any Wine with winecoreaudio.drv and ENG-11.
Test: not yet with a second real microphone: the built-in one is the only real input on the author's Mac, the rest are virtual
(asking those can hang coreaudiod). Compiles clean with the Dormison r7 build flags. To try: a game or WASAPI capture program with
WINEDEBUG=+coreaudio, plug in a headset, the driver says "input device A -> B".
Verified on: compiled only (2026-10-28); see Test.
Switch: LULLABY_AUDIO_FOLLOW=0 as a launch option turns both directions off for a run; on by default.


